PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. Clockwise direction, origin near ton

The E. coli chromosome is circular, with genes transferred linearly from an Hfr origin in a fixed direction (clockwise or counterclockwise) set by F-plasmid orientation. azi (azide-resistance/sensitivity) lies immediately adjacent to ton on the standard map (order roughly thr-leu-azi-ton-lac-gal-str). An origin placed next to ton that initiates clockwise transfer therefore sends azi into the recipient first; the next markers lie many minutes farther along the chromosome, producing precisely the observed pattern of prolonged azi expression before any other selected marker appears. The remaining choices place the origin either far from azi or in the wrong orientation, so they cannot generate early, isolated azi entry.}

The answer is A.

11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. Clockwise direction, origin near ton

The phrasing "prolonged expression of the azis gene before others" indicates that azi enters the recipient earliest and is temporally isolated from subsequent markers, which occurs only when the Hfr origin lies immediately adjacent to azi and transfer proceeds away from the neighboring gene cluster. On the standard E. coli map azi lies between leu (1.7 min) and ton/fhuA (~3.8 min). An origin placed near ton, with transfer oriented clockwise (i.e., ton → pro → lac), therefore sends azi in first and creates the largest temporal gap before the next selectable marker. All other listed origins either place azi late in the transfer order or orient transfer so that azi is not the leading marker, contradicting the observed kinetics.}

}

9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. Clockwise direction, origin near ton

The E. coli map has genes in the order thr-leu-azi-ton-lac-gal-str (clockwise). azi is therefore immediately adjacent to ton. An Hfr origin placed just proximal to ton, with the integrated F plasmid oriented so that transfer proceeds toward azi (clockwise), makes azi the first marker to enter the recipient. Because the origin lies directly next to azi and the next markers (leu, thr) lie farther away on the chromosome, a long temporal gap is observed between azi entry and the appearance of subsequent markers—exactly the pattern described. All other listed origins and directions place azi later in the transfer sequence or make it the distal rather than proximal marker, eliminating the prolonged early expression of azi.}

}

